  "query_preview": "..\n\nRemnants of overnight showers/storms vacate the area early this \nmorning as upper Midwestern high pressure builds in the from north. \nElevated portions of the weak cold front in northern Ohio are slowly \npushed south over the course of the morning which could still allow \na spotty light shower/drizzle through mid-morning along the state \nborder. Otherwise dry conditions are established across SE MI today \nwith cool northerly flow bringing temperatures back closer to \naverages, albeit still some 5 degrees warmer than mid-October \nnormals.\n\nInfluence of high pressure wanes over the course of Thursday morning \nas it tracks towards Quebec. While this occurs, a southern stream \nmid-upper trough ejects at out the 4-corners region over the central \nPlains phasing with a northern stream trough digging out of \nManitoba. Timing of lead rain has come into better focus with this \ncycle of runs with showers expected to expand southwest to northeast \nover the course of the late afternoon-evening (~20-00Z) as the \nelevated warm frontal slope lifts into SE MI. A few rumbles of \nthunder possible by late evening towards the Ohio border area as the \nsurface warm front lifts north towards M-59 allowing at least a few \nhundred J/kg of elevated instability to build over the state line. \nSurface low progged to slide directly over the area Thursday night \ncoinciding the bulk of rain coverage/QPF with rainfall totals \ngenerally falling in the 0.25-0.5\" range. \n\nSynoptic rain tapers off Friday morning as the low pushes deeper \ninto southern Ontario. That said, parent upper trough swings across \nthe west/central Great Lakes during the day ushering in significant \ncold air aloft (500mb temps fall in the -20's C) steepening lapse \nrates, which in combination with passing upper divergence, offers \nscattered convective shower chances. Increasing mixing depths within \npost-frontal cold advection looking capable to partially, if not \nfully, tap into 30-35kt flow aloft supporting wind gusts of 30-35mph \nwith potential to peak in the 35-40mph range. Core of this colder \nairmass reaches the area overnight, marked by 850mb temps falling to \naround -3C. The edge of Plains surface high pressure attempts to \nnose into southern MI Friday night as well promoting clearing skies \nand diminishing winds going into early Saturday morning. As such, \nsetup is favorable for the first freeze for a portion of SE MI as \nlows bottom in the lower 30s, especially north of M-59 where sub-\nfreezing temperatures are most likely. Below normal temperatures are \nshort-lived however as central CONUS upper ridging expands overhead \nSunday returning temps near to above normal for the first half of \nthe new week.",
